\section{Model and data}{

\eqn{IG(m, \lambda)} denotes the inverse Gaussian distribution with mean
\eqn{m} and shape \eqn{\lambda}, with density
\deqn{\sqrt{\frac{\lambda}{2 \pi x^3}} \exp\Big(-\frac{\lambda (x - m)^2}{2 m^2 x}\Big), \qquad x > 0.}
The IG process is increasing, with independent increments
\eqn{X_t - X_s \sim IG(\mu (t - s), \eta (t - s)^2)}, so that
\eqn{E[X_t] = \mu t} and \eqn{Var(X_t) = \mu^3 t / \eta}. The trajectories
are independent and have the same law; each one is observed at its own
inspection times. Let \eqn{(\Delta t_j, \Delta x_j)}, \eqn{j = 1, \dots, m},
be the durations and degradation increments (\eqn{\Delta x_j > 0}) between
two consecutive inspections of a same trajectory, gathered over all the
trajectories, \eqn{T = \sum_j \Delta t_j} and \eqn{S = \sum_j \Delta x_j}.
}

\section{Likelihood}{

With \eqn{\delta = 1 / \mu}, the log-likelihood is
\deqn{\ell(\mu, \eta) = c_0 + \frac{m}{2} \ln \eta
  - \frac{\eta}{2} \sum_j \frac{(\delta \Delta x_j - \Delta t_j)^2}{\Delta x_j},
  \qquad c_0 = \sum_j \Big[\ln \Delta t_j - \frac{1}{2} \ln(2\pi) - \frac{3}{2} \ln \Delta x_j\Big].}
}

\section{Maximum likelihood estimator}{

\enumerate{
\item Expanding the square gives
\deqn{\sum_j \frac{(\delta \Delta x_j - \Delta t_j)^2}{\Delta x_j} = \frac{(\delta S - T)^2}{S} + \hat Q,
     \qquad \hat Q = \sum_j \frac{\Delta t_j^2}{\Delta x_j} - \frac{T^2}{S} \ge 0,}
with \eqn{\hat Q = 0} if and only if the speeds
\eqn{\Delta x_j / \Delta t_j} are all equal.
\item For every \eqn{\eta}, \eqn{\ell} is therefore maximal at
\eqn{\delta = T / S}, that is \eqn{\hat\mu = S / T}.
\item The remaining function \eqn{\frac{m}{2} \ln \eta - \frac{\eta}{2} \hat Q}
is maximal at \eqn{\hat\eta = m / \hat Q}.
\item The MLE exists and is unique if and only if \eqn{\hat Q > 0}, which has
probability one as soon as \eqn{m \ge 2}. Otherwise the likelihood is
unbounded (\code{NA} with a warning).
}
}

\section{Bias}{

For \eqn{m \ge 2},
\deqn{\hat\mu \sim IG(\mu, \eta T), \qquad \eta \hat Q \sim \chi^2_{m-1},}
and \eqn{\hat Q} is independent of \eqn{S}: the conditional density of the
increments given \eqn{S} does not depend on \eqn{\mu}, and its exponent only
involves \eqn{\hat Q} (this extends a result of Tweedie (1957) to irregular
inspection times). Hence \eqn{\hat\mu} is unbiased, while, since
\eqn{E[1/V] = 1/(k - 2)} for \eqn{V \sim \chi^2_k},
\eqn{E[\hat\eta] = \frac{m}{m - 3} \eta} for \eqn{m > 3}: \eqn{\hat\eta} is
biased upwards. The function also returns its unbiased version
\deqn{\tilde\eta = \frac{m - 3}{\hat Q} = \frac{m - 3}{m} \hat\eta,}
which requires at least four increments (\code{NA} otherwise).
}

\section{Confidence intervals and estimation failures}{

With \code{ci = TRUE}, the function also returns asymptotic confidence intervals.
Let \eqn{\hat\psi} be the MLE and \eqn{\ell} the log-likelihood. The observed
Fisher information \eqn{J = -\partial^2 \ell / \partial \psi \partial \psi^\top} is
computed at \eqn{\hat\psi} by numerical differentiation (\code{\link[stats:optim]{stats::optimHess()}}),
and the standard errors are the square roots of the diagonal of
\eqn{J^{-1}}. With \eqn{z} the quantile of order \eqn{(1 + level) / 2} of the
standard normal distribution, the interval of a parameter \eqn{\psi_k} is
\itemize{
\item \eqn{\hat\psi_k \pm z\, se_k} for a real parameter (\code{mu} of the Wiener
process, \code{theta} of the variance-gamma process, \code{b} and \code{m} of the Meixner
process);
\item \eqn{\hat\psi_k \exp(\pm z\, se_k / \hat\psi_k)} for a positive parameter: the
interval \eqn{\ln \hat\psi_k \pm z\, se_k / \hat\psi_k} for \eqn{\ln \psi_k}, transformed
back, which always stays positive.
}

These intervals are asymptotic: their coverage is close to \code{level} when the
number of increments is large. The unbiased estimates \code{s2} (Wiener) and \code{eta_unbiased} (inverse
Gaussian) are given without interval. When \eqn{J} is not positive definite
(estimate close to a boundary of the parameter space), the bounds are \code{NA}.

When the maximum likelihood estimator does not exist for the data (for
instance all the speeds \eqn{\Delta x_j / \Delta t_j} equal, or a maximum at a
boundary of the parameter space), the function does not stop: it gives a
warning with the reason and returns \code{NA} estimates, with the reason in the
attribute \code{"message"}. Invalid data (times not increasing, decreasing levels
for an increasing process, too few observations) still give an error.
